1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Xun Shuang 荀爽 (128–190), ''zi'' Ciming 慈明. Late Eastern Han thinker and writer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some sources give Xun Shuang's personal name as Xu 諝. His ancestral home was Yingyin 潁陰 in Yingchuan 潁川 commandery (modern Xuchang, Henan). He reputedly was a twelfth generation descendant of Xun Kuang 荀況 (ca. 217–288). His father
2 CONC  was Xun Shu 荀淑 (83–149 or ca. 100–167?), a prominent scholar in the Yingyin area. Noted for his precociousness, Xun Shuang was able to recite the ''Chunqiu'' and ''Lun yu'' at the age of twelve. This feat earned him the praise of
2 CONC  the distinguished scholar Du Qiao 杜喬 (d. 147). In the Yingchuan area, there was a saying: “Among the Eight Dragons of the Xun clan, Ciming has no peer.” (The Eight Dragons were the eight sons of Xun Shu.) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xun Shuang did not accept any official positions until 168, when upon the recommendation of Zhao Dian 趙典 (d. 168) he was appointed gentleman of the interior. He presented a petition to the court outlining abuses and criticizing th
2 CONC e size of the imperial harem. He left office in 169 at the beginning of the Grand Proscription, which lasted until 184. During this time he went into hiding first on the seacoast, and then south to the Han River valley. He spent t
2 CONC his period engaged in scholarship and writing. 
2 CONT
2 CONT When the Proscription ended in 184, the minister of works Yuan Feng 袁逢 (d. ca. 178) recommended Xun Shuang as “Possessing the Way.” He refused all invitations to office. When Yuan Feng died, Xun Shuang observed a three-year mourni
2 CONC ng period for him. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 188, He Jin 何晉 (d. 189) dispatched an official carriage to bring Xun Shuang to the capital where he assigned him a position on his staff. He subsequently was appointed palace attendant. He Jin was assassinated by eunuchs in 189
2 CONC , and Xun Shuang left office, but was soon forced to take a position in the administration of Dong Zhuo 董卓 (d. 192). He was first assigned as administrator of Pingyuan 平原 (south of modern Pingyuan, Shandong). He traveled as far as
2 CONC  Yuanling 宛陵 (modern Xuancheng  宣城, Anhui) when he was recalled to the court to serve as chamberlain for attendants. Three days later he was named minister of works. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xun Shuang accompanied the entourage of officials that Dong Zhuo led from Luoyang to Chang'an in April of 190. In 192, he plotted with Wang Yun 王允 (137–192) to overthrow Dong Zhuo. However, Xun Shuang died of illness before Dong Z
2 CONC huo was assassinated on 22 June 192. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xun Shuang's biography in the ''Hou Han shu'' mentions that he wrote commentaries to the Classics including the ''Yi jing'', ''Shang shu'', ''Shi jing'', ''Li'', and ''Chunqiu''. He compiled the ''Han yu'' 漢語 (Remarks about the Ha
2 CONC n), which was a collection of historical events that provided moral lessons for governance. His other works included ''Gongyang wen'' 公羊問 (Questions about the Gongyang Tradition) and ''Bian chen'' 辯讖 (Debating prognostication text
2 CONC s). He also had a collection of expository essays titled ''Xin shu'' 新書  (New writings). His oeuvre reputedly amounted to 100-plus ''pian'', much of which was lost by the time of the compilation of Fan Ye's ''Hou Han shu''. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The monograph on bibliography of the ''Sui shu'' lists his collected works in one ''juan'' and a three-''juan'' collection recorded in a Liang period catalogue. The two Tang histories record his collection in two ''juan''. This wa
2 CONC s lost in the Song. Yan Kejun preserves five of his prose pieces in the ''Quan shanggu Sandai Qin Han Sanguo Liuchao wen''. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The only work of Xun Shuang's of which a significant portion survives is his commentary on the ''Zhou yi''. The monograph on bibliography of the ''Sui shu'' lists a ''Zhou yi zhu'' 周易注 in ten ''juan'' and a ''Zhou yi jiujia zhu'' 
2 CONC 周易九家注 in eleven ''juan''. Both Tang histories record a ''Zhou yi zhangju'' 周易章句 in ten ''juan'', which presumably is the same as the ''Zhou yi zhu''. This was lost in the Song, but beginning in the Qing period, scholars have recon
2 CONC structed parts of his commentary.
2 CONT
2 CONT DRK
