1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Xun Yue 荀悅 (148–209), ''zi'' Zhongyu 仲豫. Late Eastern Han historian and political thinker.
2 CONT
2 CONT Xun Yue's ancestral home was Yingyin 潁陰 in Yingchuan 潁川 commandery (modern Xuchang, Henan). Xun Yue reputedly was a thirteenth generation descendant of Xun Kuang 荀況 (ca. 217–288 BCE). Xun Yue's grandfather Xun Shu 荀淑 (83–149 or ca
2 CONC . 100–167?) was a prominent scholar in the Yingyin area. Xun Yue was a young boy when his father Xun Jian 荀儉 died. Xun Yue may have been raised by his uncle Xun Shuang 荀爽 (128–190). Xun Yue was precocious and reputedly was able to
2 CONC  explain the ''Chunqiu'' at the age of twelve. His family could not afford many books, but he was able to memorize a text quickly, and he acquired his learning by perusing the book collections of other scholars.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the reign of Emperor Ling (r. 168–189), Xun Yue refused official service on the grounds of illness. His main objection was the domination of the court by eunuchs. In 196, Cao Cao 曹操 (155–220) appointed Xun Yue to his milita
2 CONC ry staff. He later moved to Xu 許 (modern Xuchang) where he served at the court of Emperor Xian (r. 189–220). During this time he engaged in scholarly discussions with his cousin Xun Yu 荀彧 (163–212) and the scholar Kong Rong 孔融 (15
2 CONC 3–208). They reputedly were allowed to enter the emperor's private quarters where they engaged in late night conversations with him. Xun Yue subsequently served as director of the palace library and palace attendant. Although Empe
2 CONC ror Xian showed enthusiasm for Xun Yue's ideas, Cao Cao had no interest in them. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ca. 198, the emperor commissioned Xun Yue to compile a chronicle version of the history of the Former Han based on the model of the ''Zuo zhuan''. This work is known as the ''Han ji'' 漢紀 (Han annals) or ''Qian Han ji'' 前漢紀 (Former
2 CONC  Han annals). This work in thirty ''juan'' mainly consists of material Xun Yue extracted from the annals and other parts of Ban Gu's ''Han shu'' rearranged in a chronological order. In various places Xun Yue adds his own statement
2 CONC s about historical events and figures. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xun Yue also wrote a set of essays on political and moral philosophy. He presented them to Emperor Xian in 205 under the title ''Shen jian'' 申鑒 (Restating the lessons). Xun Yue explains the title as follows: “The foundation of the
2 CONC  Way is nothing but kindness and propriety. The Five Classics serve as its woof, and various other texts serve as its warp. People recite them, sing them, perform them with stringed instruments and in dance. The lessons of the pas
2 CONC t were already made clear, and in later times people restated them. Thus, the ancient sage kings with respect to kindness and propriety simply made restatements about them. Faithfully and ceaselessly making an account of them I ca
2 CONC ll it ‘Restating the Lessons.'” The received version of the ''Shen jian'' consists of only a portion of the original work. They include four titles in five chapters: “Zheng ti” 政體 (Essence of government), “Shi shi” 時事 (Current aff
2 CONC airs), “Su xian” 俗嫌 (Suspicions about contemporary mores), and “Za yan” 雜言 (Diverse remarks). The “Za yan” is divided into two sections. Much of the work concerns political thought. In the “Su xian” chapter Xun Yue expresses skept
2 CONC icism about divination and prognostication texts. The “Za yan” are written mainly in a dialogue form. 
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Xun Yue's biography in the ''Hou Han shu'', he wrote several tens of disquisitions including two pieces titled “Chong de” 崇德 (Honoring virtue) and “Zheng lun” 正論 (Disquisition on rectitude). These works were lost befo
2 CONC re the Tang.
2 CONT
